BurningDLL @PROJECT_VERSION@   (C API version 2)
Built for: @CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME@ / @CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID@ / @CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE@

Cross-platform disc-burning utility: synchronous C API, C++ wrappers, and an
asynchronous handle-based job API with live status, cancellation and full
exception containment at the DLL boundary.

Contents
--------
  bin/       burning_dll.dll | libburning_dll.so* | libburning_dll*.dylib
             burn_cli              command-line example, needs the shared lib
  lib/       burning_dll.lib       Windows import library only
  include/   burning_utility.h     public C API + C++ wrappers
  examples/  c_example.c, cpp_example.cpp, csharp_example.cs, job_example.c

Using the library
-----------------
  Add include/ to your include path, link against lib/ on Windows, and keep
  the shared library next to your executable (or on the system library path)
  at runtime. Run "burn_cli" with no arguments for the available commands.

Asynchronous jobs (recommended)
-------------------------------
  BurnJobOptions o;  memset(&o, 0, sizeof(o));
  o.size = sizeof(o);  o.mode = BURN_JOB_MODE_IMAGE;
  o.sourcePath = "disc.iso";  o.devicePath = "D:";

  BurnJobHandle h = Burn_Start(&o);          // returns immediately
  BurnJobStatus s; memset(&s, 0, sizeof(s)); s.size = sizeof(s);
  Burn_GetStatus(h, &s);                     // state / percent / phase
  Burn_Cancel(h);                            // from ANY thread
  Burn_Wait(h, -1);                          // -1 = forever, 0 = poll only
  Burn_Release(h);                           // mandatory, exactly once

  Burn_Simple(&o, &s) is the one-call blocking shortcut. BurnTask is the C++
  RAII wrapper (the destructor releases the handle). The older
  CreateISOToDisc / BurnDirectoryToDisc / CreateISOImage functions remain as
  a compatibility layer and can be interrupted with CancelCurrentBurn().

  BURN_JOB_MODE_SELFTEST runs a ~2 second cooperative job that needs no
  optical drive; use it to validate an integration ("burn_cli job-selftest"
  or "burn_cli job-selftest cancel").

  Burn_GetStatus() returns a plain snapshot with no pointers, so it stays
  valid after Burn_Release(). A released handle is rejected with
  BURN_ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE; it is never a dangling pointer.

  Every exported C function and every caller-supplied progress callback is
  wrapped in try/catch. Exceptions are reported as BURN_ERROR_EXCEPTION in
  status.errorMessage rather than unwinding through the ABI.

  No backend uses system() or a shell. Windows uses IMAPI2 with real
  DDiscFormat2DataEvents progress and CancelWrite(); Linux uses libburn's
  burn_drive_cancel(); macOS uses posix_spawnp() argv arrays and SIGTERM.

Runtime dependencies
--------------------
  Windows : IMAPI2 ships with Windows, nothing extra to distribute.
  Linux   : libburn + libisofs runtime packages must be installed on the
            target (Debian/Ubuntu: libburn4 / libisofs6 or equivalents).
  macOS   : uses the built-in hdiutil / drutil tools; no third-party runtime.

  A real optical drive is required for burning; listing devices works
  without media.

API notes
---------
  GetPlatformInfo(), GetSupportedFormats(), GetLastErrorMessage() and
  Burn_GetJobErrorMessage() return pointers to internal static buffers.
  Do NOT free them with FreeStringMemory() -- that function is only for
  heap-allocated strings returned through out-parameters.

  Set options.size = sizeof(BurnJobOptions) and status.size =
  sizeof(BurnJobStatus) before every call; this is the ABI version check.
